{"full":"miku/zek","name":"zek","description":"Generate a Go struct from XML.","category":"Developer tools","language":"Go","stars":821,"forks":63,"created":"2017-11-23T19:03:11Z","updated":"2026-04-23T13:03:10Z","license":"GPL-3.0","branch":"master","homepage":"","topics":["code-generation","hacktoberfest","xml"],"demo":null,"color":"#25392f","ink":"#d1e5c5","availability":"available","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-04T06:51:58.662Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-04T06:51:58.662Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-04T06:51:58.662Z","lastDemoCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T08:24:44.991Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-03T21:17:51.392Z","stars":821,"forks":63},{"at":"2026-10-04T06:51:58.662Z","stars":821,"forks":63}],"technologies":["Go"],"overviewAttemptAt":"2026-10-03T14:34:07.233Z","overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Zek is a prototype for creating a Go struct from an XML document. The resulting struct works best for reading XML (see also #14), to create XML, you might want to use something else.","It was developed at Leipzig University Library to shorten the time to go from raw XML to a struct that allows to access XML data in Go programs.","Mapping between XML elements and data structures is inherently flawed: an XML element is an order-dependent collection of anonymous values, while a data structure is an order-independent collection of named values."],"features":["it works fine for non-recursive structures,","does not need XSD or DTD,","it is relatively convenient to access attributes, children and text,","will generate a single struct, which make for a quite compact representation,","simple user interface,"],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/miku/zek/blob/master/README.md","fingerprint":"6f736d383a3fd34e4aac33a9a99b795cef6ae7d29aa3bf263a283f72a5bbbee5","extractedAt":"2026-10-03T07:02:42.498Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T14:34:14.974Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"Zek is a prototype for creating a Go struct from an XML document. The resulting stru